HTTPS (Hypetext Transfer Protocol Secure) is a secure version of HTTP, on port 443, which is the protocol used to transmit data between a website and a user's browser.
HTTPS uses the encryption protocol Transfer Layer Security (TSL) to encrypt the communication between the web browser and the website. When a user first connects to 
a website, the browser and site perform a 'TSL handshake'. This handshake establishes that the site is who it says it is, and establishes the public and private keys that
will be used by the connection.
To use HTTPS, a server must have a TSL certificate, which contains information about the server, including its public key and the owner of the domain. This must be
gotten from a certificate authority. To verify the server is correct, the browser will check the information present in the certificate, and verify its correctness with 
the public key. Once the browser has verified the connection, session keys are generated for encypting the communication between site and browser. This is done by 
asymmetric encryption, each site and browser generating their own temporary private and public keys. These will be used for encrypting and decrypting the data sent 
between them. To ensure that the connection isn't being tampered with (i.e a man in the middle attack), the data is signed with a Message Authentication Code (MAC)

